* ============================================================================
* workspace_multi_regression.dts
*
* MULTIPLE WORKSPACES: SIBLINGS, NESTING, AND A FILE OPEN IN TWO OF THEM.
*
* WHY THIS FILE EXISTS, GIVEN workspace_scope_regression.dts ALREADY PASSES
*   That file proved ONE relation: a scoped close spares DEFAULT. DEFAULT is
*   the ancestor of everything, so sparing it is the weakest possible evidence
*   that close is scoped -- an implementation that closed "everything except
*   area 0" would pass it. SIBLINGS are the discriminator that separates
*   "scoped to a workspace" from "scoped away from the root", and no existing
*   script opens two workspaces that are peers rather than parent and child.
*
*   WSM_T1 is that arm. WSALPHA and WSBETA are peers; WSALPHA is closed; BETA's
*   sentinel must still read. Under a close that walks anything other than its
*   own member set, BETA goes with it and this line reds.
*
* THE ARM THAT MATTERS MOST IS WSM_T2, AND IT IS ABOUT ONE FILE, NOT TWO
*   MWSHARE.DBF is opened in WSALPHA and again in WSBETA. This is the case a
*   per-workspace area registry either handles or quietly gets wrong, because
*   the two workspaces now disagree about who owns a single file handle. Close
*   WSALPHA and BETA must still read MWSHARE. If close releases by FILE rather
*   than by workspace membership, BETA loses a table it never closed, and the
*   failure is invisible in any test where each workspace holds distinct files.
*
*   This is the shape the engine already documents elsewhere: dottalkpp and
*   dottalk_bbsd share the on-disk store with no IPC, so "two openers, one
*   file" is the established condition rather than an exotic one.
*
* WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ASSERT, AND WHY
*   That a closed workspace is EMPTY, or that a closed area is gone.
*   workspace_scope_regression.dts established the rule and the reason:
*   NO MARKER IN THIS LANGUAGE CAN ASSERT THAT AN AREA IS EMPTY, because the
*   marker evaluator binds a null area unless the area is OPEN, and an ERRORED
*   MARKER PRINTS NOTHING RATHER THAN GOING RED. A suite can therefore lose a
*   claim silently and still report a full green count.
*
*   Every assertion below is consequently a POSITIVE READ of a sentinel that
*   must survive. Absence is proven only by contrast -- what is still readable
*   after an operation, never by claiming something is not.
*
* MUTATION TESTED 2026-08-22 -- THE ARMS ARE LIVE AND INDEPENDENT
*   A green suite proves nothing until an arm has been shown able to red. This
*   file had already produced one false green (see the AGAIN note below), so it
*   owed that demonstration more than most.
*
*   WSM_T1 and WSM_T2 were both mutated to expect "MUTANT" and the suite re-run
*   in a fresh process. Result: those two went .F. and THE OTHER NINE STAYED
*   .T. -- which is the outcome that matters, because it separates three cases
*   a single red cannot:
*
*     both red, nine green  -> arms are live AND independent      (observed)
*     everything red        -> arms are coupled; one break masks the rest
*     nothing red           -> the assertion never reaches the data, which is
*                              exactly what WSM_T2 was doing before WSM_G4
*                              caught it
*
*   Re-run the mutation after any change to the topology or the area map. The
*   arms are only independent for the shape they were measured against.
*
* MARKER PREFIX
*   WSM_ rather than WS_, so these can be told apart from the scope regression
*   in a combined run. WSM_G* are setup guards; if a guard reds, the arms below
*   it are meaningless and should be read as unproven rather than as passes.
*
* IDEMPOTENT PER PROCESS, NOT PER SESSION -- AND THAT IS NOT A CHOICE
*   Run this twice in ONE interactive session and the second run prints
*       WORKSPACE NEW: a workspace named WSALPHA already exists
*   three times, then proceeds against the FIRST run's topology. It passed
*   anyway on 2026-08-22 only because the shape was identical; change the
*   topology and the second run would test the old one and still report green.
*
*   The teardown cannot prevent this. WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL releases AREAS
*   everywhere -- its own help says "Close every workspace, everywhere" -- but
*   the registry still lists all four workspaces afterward at members 0, and
*   cmd_workspace.cpp exposes no DROP, DELETE or REMOVE verb for a name. Once
*   a workspace name exists in a session it cannot be reclaimed.
*
*   So: run this from datarun.ps1, which starts a fresh process each time.
*   Inside a long-lived session, run it ONCE. If you must repeat it, restart
*   the process rather than trusting the second pass.
*
* RUN
*   ./datarun.ps1 -CommandLines 'DO scripts\workspace_multi_regression.dts'
*
*   Paths given to DO/DOTSCRIPT resolve against the DATA ROOT, not the repo
*   root. The repo-relative form doubles the prefix and reports "script not
*   found" against ...\dottalkpp\data\dottalkpp\data\scripts\..., which reads
*   as a missing file rather than as a mis-rooted path. The absolute form
*   works too: DO d:\code\ccode\dottalkpp\data\scripts\workspace_multi_regression.dts
* ============================================================================

SET ECHO OFF
STOP_ON_ERROR OFF

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX

FORMULA "WORKSPACE-MULTI-PROOF-BEGIN"

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Teardown FIRST. A regression that assumes a clean tree passes for the wrong
* reason on the second run.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SET RECURSION ON
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
ERASE mwdef CONFIRM
ERASE mwalpha CONFIRM
ERASE mwbeta CONFIRM
ERASE mwgamma CONFIRM
ERASE mwshare CONFIRM

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* DEFAULT gets a sentinel, and so does the file that will be opened twice.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SELECT 0
CREATE X64 MWDEF (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 1
REPLACE MARK WITH "DEF"

SELECT 5
CREATE X64 MWSHARE (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 9
REPLACE MARK WITH "SHARE"

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Topology: DEFAULT, two PEERS, and one CHILD under the first peer.
*
*   DEFAULT
*     +-- WSALPHA          (peer)
*     |     +-- WSGAMMA    (child of WSALPHA)
*     +-- WSBETA           (peer of WSALPHA, NOT under it)
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE NEW WSALPHA
WORKSPACE NEW WSBETA
WORKSPACE NEW WSGAMMA UNDER WSALPHA

WORKSPACE SWITCH WSALPHA
SELECT 1
CREATE X64 MWALPHA (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 2
REPLACE MARK WITH "ALPHA"
* AGAIN is REQUIRED, and the first cut of this file did not have it.
* MWSHARE was created in area 5 while DEFAULT was current, so the file already
* had a work area and belonged to DEFAULT. A bare USE was REFUSED here ("already
* open in area 5"), and in WSBETA a bare USE silently RE-USED DEFAULT's area 5
* rather than opening one of its own. WSM_T2 then asserted that a file survived
* in a peer that never held it -- true by construction, proving nothing.
* WSM_G4 is what caught it. Measured 2026-08-22.
SELECT 4
USE mwshare AGAIN

WORKSPACE SWITCH WSBETA
SELECT 2
CREATE X64 MWBETA (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 3
REPLACE MARK WITH "BETA"
SELECT 6
USE mwshare AGAIN

WORKSPACE SWITCH WSGAMMA
SELECT 3
CREATE X64 MWGAMMA (ID N(4), MARK C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE ID WITH 4
REPLACE MARK WITH "GAMMA"

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* GUARDS. Every sentinel is readable before anything is closed. If any of
* these reds, treat every WSM_T* below as UNPROVEN, not as passing.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SELECT 0
GO TOP
? "WSM_G0_default_sentinel:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "DEF")
SELECT 1
GO TOP
? "WSM_G1_alpha_sentinel:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "ALPHA")
SELECT 2
GO TOP
? "WSM_G2_beta_sentinel:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "BETA")
SELECT 3
GO TOP
? "WSM_G3_gamma_sentinel:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "GAMMA")
SELECT 4
GO TOP
? "WSM_G4_share_open_in_alpha:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "SHARE")
SELECT 6
GO TOP
? "WSM_G5_share_open_in_beta:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "SHARE")

WORKSPACE REGISTRY

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* THE CLOSE. WSALPHA goes, and RECURSION ON means WSGAMMA goes with it.
* WSBETA is a PEER and must be untouched.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SET RECURSION ON
WORKSPACE SWITCH WSALPHA
WORKSPACE CLOSE

WORKSPACE REGISTRY

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* WSM_T1 -- a peer is not a descendant. This is the arm the existing scope
* regression cannot supply: sparing DEFAULT is compatible with "close
* everything but the root", sparing a SIBLING is not.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SELECT 2
GO TOP
? "WSM_T1_peer_survived_sibling_close:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "BETA")

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* WSM_T2 -- one FILE, two workspaces, one closed. BETA never closed MWSHARE
* and must still read it. A close that releases by file rather than by
* workspace membership reds here and nowhere else in this suite.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SELECT 6
GO TOP
? "WSM_T2_shared_file_survived_in_peer:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "SHARE")

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* WSM_T3 -- DEFAULT is still intact after a peer subtree was closed. Weaker
* than T1 on its own, kept because it is the arm that would catch a close
* that walked UPWARD from the closed workspace.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SELECT 0
GO TOP
? "WSM_T3_default_untouched:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "DEF")

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* WSM_T4 -- the child's FILE was released, proven by contrast: reopen it in
* the surviving peer and read the sentinel. This is how a released handle is
* demonstrated without asserting that anything is absent.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE SWITCH WSBETA
SELECT 7
USE mwgamma
GO TOP
? "WSM_T4_child_file_released_and_reopenable:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "GAMMA")

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* WSM_T5 -- SWITCH is not a no-op on state. Return to DEFAULT and read its
* sentinel without re-selecting: the workspace must restore its own area.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
SELECT 0
GO TOP
? "WSM_T5_default_readable_after_switch_back:" + (ALLTRIM(MARK) = "DEF")

* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
* Teardown. Leave the sandbox as it was found.
* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
SET RECURSION ON
WORKSPACE SWITCH DEFAULT
WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
ERASE mwdef CONFIRM
ERASE mwalpha CONFIRM
ERASE mwbeta CONFIRM
ERASE mwgamma CONFIRM
ERASE mwshare CONFIRM

FORMULA "WORKSPACE-MULTI-PROOF-END"
